00:00:02.389 --> 00:00:04.390 align:start position:0%
in a boston courtroom friday two parents  were found guilty in connection with an  elaborate nationwide college admissions  cheating scandal it was the first trial  in the so-called varsity blues case  where wealthy parents tried to get their  children into elite colleges by  falsifying extra exams and bribing  coaches to treat the children as  recruited athletes nancy chen has more  a federal jury found john wilson and  kamal abdulaziz guilty on all counts  ranging from wire fraud to bribery they  broke the law  and now they face the consequences  prosecutors say the men paid a combined  1.5 million dollars to rick singer a  college admissions consultant and the  mastermind behind an eight-year scheme  where he helped children from wealthy  families get into prestigious schools  john wilson and gamal abdelaziz used  fraud and bribery to get what they  wanted  what they did was an affront dozens of  parents and college officials have been  charged 48 people have either pleaded or  planned to plead guilty including stars  lori lockland and felicity hoffman who  both spent a short time in prison wilson  paid singer 220 thousand dollars to get  his son into usc and a million dollars  for his twin daughters to attend harvard  and stanford abdulaziz gave 300 thousand  dollars to get his daughter into usc as  a basketball recruit even though she  didn't make her varsity team in high  school prosecutors played fbi wiretap  calls during the trial to show how  abdelaziz and singer conspired together  so i just want to make sure our stories  are together i'm going to essentially  say that your 300 thousand dollar  payment  was made to our foundation to help  underserved kids okay  eric rosen the former lead prosecutor in  this case says these parents donations  cross the line the central theme from  the beginning is  if something involves lies and cheating  it's probably not legitimate and i think  the jury agreed with that here the most  serious charges in this case could mean  up to 20 years in prison with sentencing  scheduled for february for cbs saturday  morning nancy chen boston
 

